---
/**
 * foodcraft / HeroBM — "Tahanan Cafe"'s arrival.
 *
 * ROLE. The page's opening statement and its only h1. Two equal columns on the
 * white ground: on the left the underlined walnut eyebrow, a Libre Caslon Text
 * headline whose closing phrase falls into WALNUT ITALIC, a lede and two
 * actions; on the right one tall rectangular photograph at a 2px corner.
 * Lifted verbatim from the element carrying data-component="HERO" in
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html": section padding 62px 30px 0, grid
 * minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr) at 50px with align-items:center, h1
 * clamp(40px,5.4vw,74px)/1.1 with margin .26em 0 .3em, lede 17.5px/1.78 at 44ch
 * with 32px beneath it, actions 12px apart, and a 520px-tall plate on --well at
 * border-radius 2px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every path is declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts, so each is live in the sidebar AND inline):
 *   hero.image              — data-image-field, the photograph
 *   hero.kicker             — the eyebrow (.kick)
 *   hero.headline           — the h1, set:html so an owner <em> takes the
 *                             walnut italic
 *   hero.headlineLines.${i} — the SAME h1, when hero.headline holds nothing but
 *                             the derived business name; see THE HEADLINE below
 *   hero.sub                — the lede
 *   hero.cta1.text + data-href-field hero.cta1.href — the walnut filled button
 *   hero.cta2.text + data-href-field hero.cta2.href — the outlined button
 *
 * DERIVED, never stored and never editable:
 *   · the photograph's accessible name — layout.businessName and nothing else
 *   · both button hrefs, resolved through onPage() against the ids PageBM
 *     actually renders
 *   · which of the two headline paths the h1 binds to
 *   · the italic walnut tail, when the owner has not marked one (see THE
 *     SIGNATURE)
 *   · the flat variant (no photograph ⇒ one column), below
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   · No copy of any kind is written by this file. The design's demo strings
 *     ("The corner table is still yours.", "See the menu", "Contact us",
 *     "Neighbourhood café · San Juan") are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 *     template. A real café gets an arrival that says only what that café said,
 *     so every guard sits OUTSIDE its tag: no eyebrow over nothing, no empty
 *     action row, and no 520px cream slab standing in for a photograph nobody
 *     uploaded.
 *   · NO META / PILL ROW. hero.meta1, hero.meta2 and hero.trustLine are all
 *     declared by the schema and the previous template in this family renders
 *     them as three chips — but THIS design has no such row under its actions,
 *     and inventing one to use up a declared field would be a layout the
 *     designer did not draw. The fields stay editable in the sidebar and simply
 *     have no element here. (This also means R6 cannot arise outside the
 *     headline rows: there is no other list in this section.)
 *   · NO STAR ROW of any kind. The design has none here, and a star drawn from
 *     anything other than a numeric rating field is a fabricated claim. The
 *     schema's placeholder on hero.meta1 is literally '★★★★★ rated on Google';
 *     a placeholder is not a default, lib/derive-content-defaults.ts emits no
 *     meta1 for exactly that reason, and this file prints no star glyph at all.
 *   · hero.tag / hero.photos.* — not declared by the schema. A data-field on a
 *     path no sidebar input owns looks editable and focuses nothing.
 *   · .reveal. This section is above the fold; it is painted, not animated in.
 *   · An aria-label on the <section>. Naming it would make it a landmark
 *     announced with a word I wrote ("Hero"); the h1 already titles the region.
 *   · target="_blank". The design's demo markup carries it because its primary
 *     is hard-wired to a Messenger URL; here the href is the owner's and may be
 *     an in-page anchor, so navigation is left alone (the family idiom).
 *   · A restated eyebrow. The underline IS the design's signature and it lives
 *     on the shared .kick primitive — a section that re-declares the eyebrow
 *     loses the border-bottom. Only a wrap safety is added, and in two places:
 *     max-width:100% in the base rule, overflow-wrap at 520px.
 *
 * THE HEADLINE, and why one heading reads two paths. This design's headline is
 * ONE flowing sentence whose closing phrase falls into walnut italic — not a
 * stack of lines — so the h1 is hero.headline rendered through set:html and the
 * emphasis is an <em> the OWNER types.
 * The catch is WHICH FIELD actually holds the owner's headline, because two
 * layers upstream disagree:
 *   · lib/derive-content-defaults.ts auto-fills hero.headline with the BARE
 *     BUSINESS NAME on every named site (`headline: name || undefined`), and
 *     lib/astro-builder.ts's mergeShallow lets that derived value stand
 *     wherever the owner left the field unset.
 *   · lib/services/groq.service.ts — the extraction that writes the copy for a
 *     real submission — emits hero.headlineLines and NEVER hero.headline.
 * So "always prefer hero.headline" prints the business name and drops the
 * authored headline on the floor for exactly the sites that have one. The rule
 * is therefore: hero.headline wins whenever it is AUTHORED, meaning anything
 * other than the business name the derive layer fills in. When it is that
 * auto-fill (or empty), the surviving headlineLines rows win — flowed INLINE
 * inside the same h1, because this design's headline is a sentence and not a
 * stack — each carrying its ORIGINAL array index, so dropping a blank row 2
 * cannot misdirect an edit into row 3 (R6).
 * The field a click opens is always the field the visible text came from:
 * binding hero.headlineLines.0 to text that arrived as hero.headline (or the
 * reverse) aims an inline edit at a slot the sidebar is not showing.
 *
 * THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE. The design's h1 is two-tone — ink, then a
 * walnut italic tail ("is still yours.") — and it got there because the
 * DESIGNER typed <em> into their own markup. No owner is going to type a tag
 * into a text box, so without a fallback the headline renders one flat colour
 * on every real site and one of the two things that makes this template
 * recognisable never ships. When the owner has NOT marked emphasis, the tail is
 * derived from THEIR OWN sentence and adds not one word — it only decides where
 * the sentence turns. An explicit <em> always wins; this fills a silence.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). The arrival renders when it has something to SAY or SHOW:
 * a headline, a lede, or the photograph. It deliberately does NOT count the
 * eyebrow (derive-content-defaults builds one from city + trade for sites with
 * neither name, tagline nor upload) and does NOT count the two buttons (derive
 * sets cta1/cta2 text UNCONDITIONALLY, so counting either makes the gate a
 * no-op that always passes).
 * FOR REVIEW: this is the one section in the family whose gate counts the
 * headline, because here the headline IS the body — it is the document's only
 * h1, and a hero dropped for having "only" an h1 takes the page's heading with
 * it and opens the site on the trust row.
 *
 * NO PHOTOGRAPH ⇒ ONE COLUMN. The plate is a COLUMN here, not a background. If
 * the owner uploaded nothing, rendering it anyway leaves a 520px cream slab
 * beside the copy — a hole at full height. So the plate is dropped entirely and
 * the grid collapses to a single track, with the copy held at 555px: that is
 * the width the left column actually has at the design's own measure
 * ((1160 − 50) / 2), so the sentence is not re-flowed to a width the design
 * never gave it.
 *
 * THE SECTION HAS NO BOTTOM PADDING, and that is the design, not an oversight.
 * The HERO element ends at `padding:62px 30px 0`; the TRUST row below carries
 * its own 56px of top margin, and every other section brings its own top
 * padding from the wrapper's `section` rule. A floor here would double it.
 *
 * COLOUR / CONTRAST (R1 — no literal hex in this file, only PageBM's tokens):
 *   --ink headline on --paper 16.91:1 · --ink-soft lede 7.71:1 · --accent
 *   eyebrow and italic 7.11:1 · --on-accent on the walnut fill 7.11:1 · --ink
 *   label on --paper inside a --line-2 hairline 16.91:1. Every pairing is the
 *   design's own, taken from the wrapper's token table unchanged.
 *   THESE NUMBERS ARE READ OFF THE ONE COMPUTED TABLE, NOT WORKED OUT BY HAND,
 *   and they AGREE with the wrapper figure for figure — PageBM's VERIFIED
 *   CONTRAST block gives the same 16.91 / 7.71 / 7.11 for these three pairs.
 *   An earlier draft of this comment asserted that the wrapper's whole table
 *   ran low and that its --band-kick / --band figure ran high; neither was
 *   true, and the disagreement was hand arithmetic done in this file. If a hex
 *   ever moves, re-run the table and copy from it rather than recomputing in a
 *   section file.
 *   The 2px corner is --r; this file states no radius of its own, because the
 *   design's hero radius IS the wrapper's --r and .plate already carries it.
 *
 * CLASS-NAME LAW (not inferable, do not "tidy"): the section is .tc-arrival,
 * NOT .hero — lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts force-paints `.hero .btn-ghost` with
 * !important and would strand a label here. Buttons are .btn + .btn--fill /
 * .btn--line (double dash) for the same reason. Never .btn-primary /
 * .btn-ghost / .btn-yellow / .btn-light / .nav-cta / .trust / .testi /
 * .cta-band in this family. The `tc-` prefix is this template's and is not
 * shared with the BL sibling's `kc-`: an audit had to rename a whole set of
 * classes that were copied verbatim from the template before it.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content, layout } = Astro.props;

// Anchor guard, copied from the BL sibling and re-pointed at THIS page.
// lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes '#visit' for hero.cta1 and
// '#services' for hero.cta2; this page renders `visit` but not `services`, so
// one is kept and the other is remapped. Resolve every in-page href against
// what PageBM actually draws: keep it when the id exists, remap it when this
// design carries the same block under another id, otherwise fall back to the
// target the design was built around. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http)
// pass through untouched.
// Keep PAGE_ANCHORS and ANCHOR_ALIAS in step with PageBM — and with the copies
// in HeaderBM, CtaBandBM and FooterBM. There is no shared import by design (a
// section component in this repo receives only `content` + `layout`); the
// copies must stay identical by hand.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  location: 'visit', contact: 'visit',
  services: 'menu', rooms: 'menu',
  host: 'about', why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
  book: 'main',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[href.slice(1)];
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(href.slice(1))) return href;
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};

// Display fallbacks only — the HOOK below is always hero.image, the path the
// sidebar's image picker writes. The quote escape keeps a filename with an
// apostrophe from closing the url('') early and killing the declaration.
const heroImage = String(hero.image || (hero.photos && hero.photos[0]) || (content.photos && content.photos[0]) || '');
const heroImageCss = heroImage.replace(/'/g, '%27');

const kicker = hero.kicker || '';

// THE HEADLINE — full reasoning in the doc comment. hero.headline is this
// design's field; the rows take over only when hero.headline is nothing but the
// business name derive-content-defaults auto-filled (or is empty), which is the
// shape every AI-written submission arrives in.
const bizName = String(layout?.businessName ?? '').trim();
const headline = String(hero.headline ?? '').trim();
const lineRows: Array<{ text: string; i: number }> = (Array.isArray(hero.headlineLines) ? hero.headlineLines : [])
  .map((t: any, i: number) => ({ text: String(t ?? ''), i }))
  .filter((l: { text: string }) => l.text.trim() !== '');
// …and only when they SAY something hero.headline does not. On the ordinary
// named site both hold the same one line, and the single-line hook keeps it.
const useRows =
  lineRows.length > 0 &&
  (headline === '' || headline === bizName) &&
  lineRows.map((l) => l.text.trim()).join(' ') !== headline;
const hasHeadline = useRows || headline !== '';

// ── THE SIGNATURE, MADE REACHABLE ────────────────────────────────────────
// When the owner has NOT marked emphasis themselves, the tail is derived from
// their OWN sentence and adds not one word:
//   • more than one line                  -> the last line
//   • a comma, dash, colon or semicolon   -> everything after the LAST one
//   • anything else                       -> nothing at all
// That last case is deliberate. A single clause is a single clause, and
// splitting inside one would lean on a word the owner never leaned on — the
// design's own headline turns at "table / is still yours" precisely because
// that is where its sentence turns, and only the writer knows that point.
const hasOwnEm = (v: string): boolean => /<em[\s>]/i.test(v);
const emphasise = (raw: string): string => {
  if (!raw || hasOwnEm(raw)) return raw;
  const lines = raw.split(/\r?\n/).map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
  if (lines.length > 1) {
    const tail = lines.pop() as string;
    return lines.join(' ') + ' <em>' + tail + '</em>';
  }
  // The LAST break wins, so a sentence with two commas emphasises its close
  // rather than its middle. The lookahead keeps a trailing comma from matching.
  const re = /[,;:—–]\s+(?=\S)/g;
  let cut = -1;
  let m: RegExpExecArray | null;
  while ((m = re.exec(raw)) !== null) cut = m.index + m[0].length;
  if (cut <= 0 || cut >= raw.length) return raw;
  return raw.slice(0, cut) + '<em>' + raw.slice(cut) + '</em>';
};
const headlineHtml = emphasise(headline);
// The rows path marks its LAST row instead, and only when no row is already
// carrying the owner's own <em>.
const rowsOwnEm = lineRows.some((l) => hasOwnEm(l.text));

const sub = hero.sub || '';
const cta1Text = hero.cta1?.text || '';
// The design's primary is its contact action and its secondary points at the
// menu; those are the two fallbacks when an owner's href resolves to nothing.
const cta1Href = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#visit');
const cta2Text = hero.cta2?.text || '';
const cta2Href = onPage(hero.cta2?.href, '#menu');

// The photograph's accessible name is the owner's own words or nothing. With no
// business name there is no honest name for the picture, so it leaves the
// accessibility tree instead of announcing a phrase I invented. Built as ONE
// spread object so a conditional attribute can never end up beside a literal
// (R9 — two components in an earlier family shipped exactly that duplication,
// and the literal aria-hidden won).
const photoA11y: Record<string, string> = bizName
  ? { role: 'img', 'aria-label': bizName }
  : { 'aria-hidden': 'true' };

const hasArrival = !!(hasHeadline || sub || heroImage);
---
{hasArrival && (
<section class={heroImage ? 'tc-arrival' : 'tc-arrival tc-arrival--flat'}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="tc-ar-grid">
      <div class="tc-ar-copy">
        {kicker && <span class="kick tc-ar-kick" data-field="hero.kicker">{kicker}</span>}
        {!useRows && headline !== '' && <h1 class="tc-ar-h1" data-field="hero.headline" set:html={headlineHtml}></h1>}
        {useRows && (
          <h1 class="tc-ar-h1">
            {lineRows.map((l, k) => (
              <span class:list={['tc-ar-ln', !rowsOwnEm && k === lineRows.length - 1 && 'tc-ar-ln--em']}><span data-field={`hero.headlineLines.${l.i}`} set:html={l.text}></span>{k === lineRows.length - 1 ? '' : ' '}</span>
            ))}
          </h1>
        )}
        {sub && <p class="lede tc-ar-lede" data-field="hero.sub">{sub}</p>}
        {(cta1Text || cta2Text) && (
          <div class="tc-ar-ctas">
            {cta1Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--fill" href={cta1Href} data-field="hero.cta1.text" data-href-field="hero.cta1.href">{cta1Text}</a>
            )}
            {cta2Text && (
              <a class="btn btn--line tc-ar-line" href={cta2Href} data-field="hero.cta2.text" data-href-field="hero.cta2.href">{cta2Text}</a>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
      </div>

      {/* The photograph. No upload ⇒ no column at all, rather than a 520px
          cream slab standing in for a picture nobody supplied. */}
      {heroImage && (
        <div class="plate tc-ar-shot">
          <div
            class="ph tc-ar-ph"
            data-image-field="hero.image"
            style={`background-image:url('${heroImageCss}');`}
            {...photoA11y}
          ></div>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBM's token vocabulary
     (--paper --well --ink --ink-soft --accent --line-2 --disp --sans --r) and
     from its shared primitives (.wrap .kick .lede .btn .btn--fill .btn--line
     .plate .ph). There is no literal hex in this file, and no radius either:
     this design's hero corner IS --r, which .plate already carries.

     HOW THESE RULES WIN, and the one config that would break them. Astro scopes
     a style block with the project's `scopedStyleStrategy`, and this project
     takes the default, 'attribute' — so `.tc-ar-shot` ships as
     `.tc-ar-shot[data-astro-cid-…]`, specificity (0,2,0). The wrapper's
     primitives are `html[data-page="BM"] :where(.plate)`, specificity (0,1,1),
     because :where() contributes nothing; its `section` rule is (0,1,2). Two
     classes beat one, which is how the plate's height replaces the 180px floor,
     the h1 keeps its .26em margins against the shared `margin:0`, the lede
     keeps its 32px floor, and the section keeps its 62px/0 padding — none of it
     with !important.
     Setting scopedStyleStrategy:'where' in astro.config.mjs would collapse
     every one of those to (0,1,0) and silently hand the wrapper the win. Two-
     class rules (`.tc-ar-ctas .btn`) survive that; the single-class ones do not. */

  /* Design: max-width:1160px; margin:0 auto; padding:62px 30px 0. The measure
     and the gutter are .wrap's job; the 62px top and the ZERO floor are this
     section's. See the doc comment on why there is no bottom padding. */
  .tc-arrival {
    padding: 62px 0 0;
  }

  /* Design: grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr); gap:50px;
     align-items:center. The grid is the one place this design uses a gap rather
     than a rule — everywhere else it draws with borders, but the hero's two
     halves are not a table and the design gives them air. */
  .tc-ar-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 50px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* No photograph ⇒ one track, and the copy holds the measure the left column
     actually has at the design's own width ((1160 − 50) / 2). Letting the
     sentence run the full 1160 would be a headline this design never draws. */
  .tc-arrival--flat .tc-ar-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .tc-arrival--flat .tc-ar-copy { max-width: 555px; }

  /* min-width:0 lets a long word wrap inside the track instead of widening it
     and pushing the page sideways. */
  .tc-ar-copy { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE EYEBROW IS NOT RESTATED. Its 10px/.34em caps, its walnut and — above
     all — its border-bottom + 9px of padding live on the shared .kick, and a
     redeclaration here would drop the underline that is the design's single
     most recognisable mark. Only the block behaviour is touched, and only so a
     very long derived kicker can wrap instead of stretching the column. */
  .tc-ar-kick { max-width: 100%; }

  /* Design: font-size:clamp(40px,5.4vw,74px); line-height:1.1;
     margin:.26em 0 .3em. Larger and tighter than the shared .h2, which is why
     this is an h1 rule of its own and not that primitive. The design sets NO
     max-width on the h1 — its column is the measure — so none is invented
     here; the narrow queries below add one only once the columns collapse. */
  .tc-ar-h1 {
    font-family: var(--disp);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(40px, 5.4vw, 74px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    margin: .26em 0 .3em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  /* THE SIGNATURE. Libre Caslon Text ships a real 400 italic, so this is a
     drawn italic and not a synthesised oblique. :global() because Astro cannot
     scope markup that arrived through set:html (R3) — which covers both the
     owner's own <em> and the derived tail on the single-line path. */
  .tc-ar-h1 :global(em) { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
  /* Rows path only (hero.headlineLines). They FLOW as one sentence rather than
     stacking, because this design's h1 is a sentence — the wrap comes from the
     column, exactly as it does on the single-line path. The word space between
     rows is a real text node in the markup, not a faked margin. */
  .tc-ar-ln { display: inline; }
  /* The derived tail on the rows path. Same treatment as an owner-typed <em>,
     declared separately because the rows are real elements this file owns and
     so do not need :global(). */
  .tc-ar-ln--em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }

  /* Design: the lede's 17.5px/1.78/44ch is the shared .lede verbatim (its clamp
     tops out at exactly 17.5px); only the 32px beneath it is local. */
  .tc-ar-lede { margin: 0 0 32px; }

  /* Design: display:flex; gap:12px; flex-wrap:wrap; align-items:center. */
  .tc-ar-ctas {
    display: flex;
    gap: 12px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* The design's outlined secondary is the shared button at 28px of side
     padding rather than 30px — the only metric in which the two differ. */
  .tc-ar-line { padding-left: 28px; padding-right: 28px; }

  /* Design: height:520px; the plate colour (which is --well), a 2px corner
     (which is --r) and overflow:hidden — that is the .plate primitive exactly,
     so only the height is stated here. The wrapper's .plate reserves a 180px
     minimum for short plates; this one states its own height, so that floor is
     released. */
  .tc-ar-shot {
    height: 520px;
    min-height: 0;
  }
  /* Two classes deep on purpose: the wrapper's `:where(.plate) .ph` scores one
     element more than a single scoped class would, so `.tc-ar-ph` alone would
     lose to it. Restating cover/center costs nothing and means the photograph
     cannot letterbox if that primitive is ever narrowed. No colour is restated —
     that stays in the primitive, where the theme engine keeps control of it. */
  .tc-ar-shot .tc-ar-ph {
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  /* ── R8 · this section's OWN grid. The wrapper handles the shared 760/520
     pass; the two columns are this file's problem and collapse here.

     THE CLAMP LADDER, checked across breakpoints because an audit on the
     previous template found a headline that GREW as the viewport narrowed. The
     base clamp(40px,5.4vw,74px) is already at its 40px FLOOR by 741px, so it is
     left untouched at 900 — there is nothing to re-scale, and any cap written
     here would have to sit above 48.6px (its value at 900px) to avoid a jump.
     The 520 query then caps at exactly 40px, which is what the base clamp is
     handing over at that width, so the ramp only ever descends: 74 → 40 → 29. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .tc-ar-grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 34px;
    }
    /* Full width now, so the sentence takes a measure instead of a column. */
    .tc-ar-h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
    .tc-arrival--flat .tc-ar-copy { max-width: none; }
    .tc-ar-lede { margin-bottom: 28px; }
    /* Stacked, the plate is now full width, so 520px of it would be a wall
       between the copy and the row below. */
    .tc-ar-shot { height: clamp(360px, 52vw, 460px); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* Nothing may push the page sideways at 360px: long unbroken strings break
       anywhere, and a long uppercase label wraps inside a full-width button
       instead of widening the row. */
    .tc-arrival { padding-top: 46px; }
    .tc-ar-grid { gap: 28px; }
    .tc-ar-kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .tc-ar-h1 { font-size: clamp(29px, 8.6vw, 40px); max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    .tc-ar-lede { max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; margin-bottom: 24px; }
    .tc-ar-ctas { gap: 10px; }
    /* white-space:normal lets a label wrap at its spaces; overflow-wrap covers
       the label that HAS none. Without the second declaration one long unbroken
       word runs straight out of the button box at 360px — the wrapper's
       overflow-x:hidden suppresses the scrollbar and the label is clipped
       instead, which is the same bug wearing a different coat. */
    .tc-ar-ctas .btn { flex: 1 1 100%; white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; text-align: center; }
    /* Re-join the wrapper's 520 button padding: without this the rule above
       keeps the secondary at 28px while the primary drops to 22px. */
    .tc-ar-line { padding-left: 22px; padding-right: 22px; }
    /* The CAP meets the 360px floor of the query above rather than clearing it:
       at a larger cap the photograph would get TALLER as the viewport crossed
       520px downward, which is a plate growing on a smaller screen. */
    .tc-ar-shot { height: clamp(300px, 78vw, 360px); }
  }
</style>
